7",null,{"onMount":true,"delay":0.2,"y":12,"children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"head-lede","children":"Find the gap, tear down the competitor, write the PRD, kill the vibecoded look, then solve the revenue equation backwards. Five prompts, each one feeding the next."}],null]}]]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"cover","children":[["$","img",null,{"src":"/covers/5-prompts-zero-to-10k.webp","width":1200,"height":800,"alt":"","fetchPriority":"high","decoding":"async"}],["$","canvas",null,{"aria-hidden":"true"}]]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"sheet","children":[["$","canvas",null,{"className":"seam","data-seam":"#F5F2EA","aria-hidden":"true"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"pad sheet-in","children":[["$","article",null,{"className":"read guide","children":["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Most people start with an idea. Start with other people's money instead. Somebody in your category is already charging for something and already annoying their users about it. That gap is your product, and you can find it in an afternoon."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Five prompts below. Gap, teardown, PRD, design, funnel. Copy each one into Claude, fill the brackets, and don't skip ahead. Every prompt produces a document the next one eats."}],["$","h2","2",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"Prompt 1: Find the gap"}],["$","p","3",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"You're looking for apps that make real money from users who are actively irritated. High revenue plus low rating is a market telling you exactly what it wants and not getting it."}],["$","div","4",{"className":"mt-5","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-2 flex items-center justify-between gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[12px] text-ink-dim","children":"Copy and paste"}],["$","$L17",null,{"text":"Pull the App Store top charts for [CATEGORY], free, paid, and top grossing.\nI want the top 100 from each. For every app, get me: price model, rating,\nrating count, and how long it's been on the chart.\n\nThen find the mismatches. I'm hunting for apps that rank high on the GROSSING\nchart but sit under 4.2 stars, or whose recent reviews keep complaining about\nthe same three things: price, ads, a missing feature, whatever it is. Those\nare apps making real money from users who are actively annoyed.\n\nGive me a ranked table: app, what it charges, the complaint pattern from its\nlast ~50 reviews, and a one-line gap I could exploit. Rank by\n(revenue signal x user frustration). I want 5 targets, not 50."}]]}],"$L18"]}],"$L19","$L1a","$L1b","$L1c","$L1d","$L1e","$L1f","$L20","$L21","$L22","$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26","$L27","$L28","$L29","$L2a","$L2b"]}]}],"$L2c"]}]]}],"$L2d"]
18:["$","pre",null,{"className":"rounded-xl bg-[#0a1020] p-5 font-mono text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[#e4e9f5] overflow-x-auto","children":["$","code",null,{"children":"Pull the App Store top charts for [CATEGORY], free, paid, and top grossing.\nI want the top 100 from each. For every app, get me: price model, rating,\nrating count, and how long it's been on the chart.\n\nThen find the mismatches. I'm hunting for apps that rank high on the GROSSING\nchart but sit under 4.2 stars, or whose recent reviews keep complaining about\nthe same three things: price, ads, a missing feature, whatever it is. Those\nare apps making real money from users who are actively annoyed.\n\nGive me a ranked table: app, what it charges, the complaint pattern from its\nlast ~50 reviews, and a one-line gap I could exploit. Rank by\n(revenue signal x user frustration). I want 5 targets, not 50."}]}]
19:["$","p","5",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Five targets is the point, because a list of fifty is a list you never act on."}]
1a:["$","h2","6",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"Prompt 2: The teardown"}]
1b:["$","p","7",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Pick one target off that list. Now read every angry review they've ever gotten. Their one-star reviews are your feature spec, written by people who already pay for this category."}]
1c:["$","div","8",{"className":"mt-5","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-2 flex items-center justify-between gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[12px] text-ink-dim","children":"Copy and paste"}],["$","$L17",null,{"text":"Deep-dive [TARGET APP]. Read its most recent 200 App Store reviews and cluster\nthe complaints: what do people hate, what do they beg for, what made them cancel.\nThen map the paywall: every feature behind it, and exactly what it costs monthly\nand yearly.\n\nNow build the undercut plan:\n1. Which 20% of their features drive 80% of the value. That's my MVP, nothing else.\n2. Which paid feature I should give away FREE, the one that makes their users\n   feel dumb for paying.\n3. My price: aggressive enough to hurt them, high enough that $10k/mo doesn't\n   need 10,000 users. Show the math at 2-3 price points.\n4. A positioning one-liner: \"[Their app] but [the thing their reviews beg for].\"\n\nOne page. If a feature doesn't answer a complaint from their reviews, it doesn't exist."}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"rounded-xl bg-[#0a1020] p-5 font-mono text-[13px] leading-[1.7] text-[#e4e9f5] overflow-x-auto","children":["$","code",null,{"children":"Deep-dive [TARGET APP]. Read its most recent 200 App Store reviews and cluster\nthe complaints: what do people hate, what do they beg for, what made them cancel.\nThen map the paywall: every feature behind it, and exactly what it costs monthly\nand yearly.\n\nNow build the undercut plan:\n1. Which 20% of their features drive 80% of the value. That's my MVP, nothing else.\n2. Which paid feature I should give away FREE, the one that makes their users\n   feel dumb for paying.\n3. My price: aggressive enough to hurt them, high enough that $10k/mo doesn't\n   need 10,000 users. Show the math at 2-3 price points.\n4. A positioning one-liner: \"[Their app] but [the thing their reviews beg for].\"\n\nOne page. If a feature doesn't answer a complaint from their reviews, it doesn't exist."}]}]]}]
1d:["$","div","9",{"className":"mt-6 rounded-xl border border-accent/30 bg-accent/10 px-5 py-4 leading-[1.7] text-ink","children":"The free feature matters more than the price. Pick the one thing their users resent paying for, give it away, and every review of their app becomes an ad for yours."}]
1e:["$","h2","10",{"className":"mt-12 t-h3 text-ink","children":"Prompt 3: The PRD, in plan mode"}]
1f:["$","p","11",{"className":"mt-5 text-lg leading-[1.75] text-ink","children":"Don't tell Claude to build you an app. Make it write the PRD first, and keep it in plan mode so nothing gets built that you haven't approved. This is the difference between a project you can still maintain in week six and a pile of code you're scared to open."}]
2e:T497,You're my lead engineer and PM. We're building [APP], [one-liner from Prompt 2].
Use plan mode for this entire project: before ANY code gets written, at every
phase, you present a plan and wait for my approval. Never skip that step, even
when the next move feels obvious.

Step one is a PRD, saved as PRD.md. This becomes the source of truth for
everything after:
- Problem and the one-liner from the teardown
- Target user and the exact complaint we're solving (quote the reviews)
- The ONE core loop a user repeats daily
- Every screen, the data model, and what's behind the paywall
- Non-goals: the features we are explicitly NOT building, so scope can't creep
- Success metrics: what numbers tell us the loop works

Stack: React Native + Expo, Supabase for auth and data. Boring on purpose,
a solo dev has to maintain this.

Once I approve the PRD, plan then build in this order: core loop, onboarding,
paywall. Each phase: plan first, my sign-off, then code, then tests green before
we move on. Anything tempting that's not in the PRD goes in FUTURE.md, not in
the app. Real empty and error states on every screen, and when something fails
the copy names the real cause.2f:T497,You're my lead engineer and PM. We're building [APP], [one-liner from Prompt 2].
Use plan mode for this entire project: before ANY code gets written, at every
phase, you present a plan and wait for my approval. Never skip that step, even
